STEP 5 - The JDK major, and proof that the host COMMITS the heap
  Every Joern invocation runs under major 21, the version the pinned Joern
  release documents as its tested requirement; a wrong major is a wrong
  assignment and halts. The major is read from the JVM's own property rather
  than eyeballed off a banner.

  The heap is then proved committable rather than merely reservable: -Xms64g
  with -Xmx64g and -XX:+AlwaysPreTouch touches every page at startup, so an exit
  status of 0 is proof the host can commit 64 GiB, which is strictly stronger
  than reserving it. The rule is one-way: raising a heap is permitted and
  reported, lowering one is not, because a truncated result's silence cannot be
  told apart from a clean one. No value above 64 GiB was used by this
  invocation, so no further commit proof is owed - PHASE 3 records the value the
  query actually received.

