STEP 7 - Sequencing: nothing else was holding a 64 GB Joern process
  The probe follows normalization rather than branching off the graph, so that
  only ONE 64 GB Joern process is ever live: the Stage 3 Joern runner and this
  probe would otherwise contend for the host's entire memory. The evidence is
  the presence of the records those earlier stages own - the Stage 3 runner's
  status file and the normalizer's and adapter-tests' run records - together
  with a live check for a Joern process. Their contents are NOT restated here
  and no figure of theirs enters this file; only their existence is the
  sequencing fact. The listing was taken after the invocation, and its own
  modification times are what carry the ordering: all three records predate the
  09:34:39.945Z start in PHASE 3.

  The first of the two PROCESS checks below is honest about its own defect and
  it is kept rather than quietly replaced: a pgrep pattern of "joern|jimple2cpg" matches the
  evidence collector's own command line, so its exit status of 0 is a SELF-MATCH
  and not a live Joern process. The second check, taken after the invocation
  with an exact process-name match, is the one that measures the condition; the
  four-line ps listing beside it shows the largest processes on the host with no
  JVM among them.

