================================================================================
PHASE 4 - WHAT THE QUERY REPORTED
================================================================================

  Every figure in this phase is cited to the line of APPENDIX B that carries it,
  and APPENDIX B is the console stream the query itself wrote, verbatim. Nothing
  here is a second measurement of anything: it is the one measurement, indexed.

  4.1 COMPILE STATUS AND RUN STATUS

    compile status : compiled                                  [APPENDIX B:106]
    run status     : completed                                 [APPENDIX B:107]
    exit status    : 0                                         [PHASE 3 FIELD 1]

    The convention behind the compile field, stated so it is not read as a
    self-certification. The field is written BY THE RUNNING SCRIPT, so its
    presence is itself the evidence: a compile failure produces no result region
    and no result files at all, and the compiler's diagnostic lands in the
    console stream instead. The marker protocol makes the distinction visible
    from the outside - a completed run ends ---BLITZY-OK--- (APPENDIX B:122), a
    failed one prints ---BLITZY-FAILURE--- and emits no result region, because a
    partial result region is worse than none: it looks like a completed run.

  4.2 THE GRAPH AS LOADED

    load mechanism : importCpg into the switched workspace     [APPENDIX B:31-34]
    workspace      : queries/joern/.workspace                  [APPENDIX B:32-33]
    methods        : 1 397 339                                 [APPENDIX B:36]
    type decls     : 119 691                                   [APPENDIX B:37]
    files          : 45 037                                    [APPENDIX B:38]

    Methods greater than zero is the load's own pass condition; 1 397 339 is
    that measurement and not a restatement of an earlier stage's count.

  4.3 RETURNED RECORDS, AND DISTINCT ROUTES RATHER THAN SUMMED RETURNS

    returned record count : 4                                  [APPENDIX B:108]
    of which boundary     : 4                                  [APPENDIX B:98]
    of which route        : 0                                  [APPENDIX B:98]
    total-returns cap     : 256, not reached                   [APPENDIX B:98]
    DISTINCT ROUTES       : 0                                  [APPENDIX B:109]

    Distinct routes is 0, and it is a DEDUPLICATED figure rather than a sum. The
    query runs two walks over the same handler/sink pair - one that expands
    dynamic-dispatch fan-out sites and one that records them without expanding -
    and their routes are deduplicated on (entry point, sink host, hop sequence)
    and counted once, never added together [APPENDIX B:70-72]. Here both walks
    returned 0, so the distinction does not change the arithmetic; it is stated
    because a formulation that returned routes on both walks would otherwise be
    double-counted, and the convention has to be the same either way.

    A zero here is the capability finding, reported as measured. It was not
    turned into a non-zero by loosening or removing the bound, and 4.5 records
    what the boundary measurements say about it.

  4.4 THE BOUND, AND WHETHER IT WAS REACHED - REPORTED AS MEASURED

    bound value (MAX_CALL_DEPTH) : 12                          [APPENDIX B:112]
    bound reached                : TRUE                        [APPENDIX B:113]

    "Reported as measured" is the whole point of this row. A result came back and
    the bound was STILL reached; the flag is read off the walk counters rather
    than inferred from the run having completed. Which bound bit, per walk
    [APPENDIX B:70-71]:

      walk A-follows-fan-out   depth_bound_reached=true  max_depth=12
                               expansions 25 009 of 200 000 per entry point
                               call sites considered 33 565
                               fan-out sites seen 86, not followed 0
                               budget_exhausted=false  route_cap_reached=false
                               routes 0
      walk B-fan-out-recorded  depth_bound_reached=true  max_depth=12
                               expansions 5 598 of 200 000 per entry point
                               call sites considered 11 575
                               fan-out sites seen 55, not followed 55
                               budget_exhausted=false  route_cap_reached=false
                               routes 0

    So exactly one of the three bounds bit, in both walks: the depth bound, with
    a frontier still non-empty at depth 12. The expansion budget and the route
    cap were not reached in either walk.

    What a reached depth bound does and does not license anyone to conclude. On
    its own it leaves open whether a deeper walk would have reached a sink host.
    What settles that here is the boundary measurement in 4.5 rather than the
    bound: three of the four hops on this route are not CALL edges at all, and no
    increase in depth introduces an edge that does not exist. The bound is
    therefore reported as reached, and the absence of a route is attributed to
    the measured boundaries. The bound was not raised and the query was not
    re-run - a raised bound is a DIFFERENT query and would be reported as one.

    The other bounds this query carries, all named in its source and none of
    them implicit: MAX_ROUTES 64, MAX_EXPANSIONS_PER_ENTRY 200 000,
    MAX_TOTAL_RETURNS 256, MAX_ENTRY_POINTS 16, MAX_CALL_SCAN 200 000,
    FANOUT_CALLEE_THRESHOLD 32. Every traversal in the query is bounded; none
    runs unbounded.

