  4.9 WHETHER AN EXPECTED-SPURIOUS ROUTE WAS ABSENT

    THE DEFINITION, narrow and mechanical, and it judges THE QUERY: a route is
    spurious ONLY where the handler does pass an authorization or ACL predicate
    before reaching the sink, the predicate set being exactly the five named
    selectors listed in 4.8 - aclsEnabled, checkAdminPermissions,
    checkModifyPermissions, checkUIViewPermissions and isAuthenticationEnabled -
    on the single source type org.apache.spark.SecurityManager. Nothing else
    makes a route spurious under this definition.

    spurious routes           : 0 of 0                                    [B:94]
    expected-spurious absent  : TRUE                                      [B:95]
    absence basis             : STRUCTURAL                            [B:96, B:111]

    The basis distinction is the finding, not a footnote. "Structural" means no
    call site of any of the five named predicates exists on the route surface at
    all - 0 of the 18 graph-wide call sites are there - so no route COULD have
    passed one. The absence is therefore not evidence that the query filtered
    well: with an emitted set of 0 routes, a spurious count of 0 would have been
    arithmetically unavoidable regardless. Reporting it as "filtering" would have
    credited the query with a discrimination it never had to exercise.

    This is a statement about THIS QUERY'S OWN OUTPUT under THIS QUERY'S OWN
    definition of the term. It is not an assessment of Spark, of any Spark
    component or of any Spark configuration, and nothing here should be read as
    one.

  4.10 DUPLICATE-FORMULATION STATUS, AGAINST EVERY OTHER QUERY

    duplicate formulation : not_duplicate                     [B:116]

    The probe has three queries, so this query is compared against the other
    two, individually:

      against 02-dataflow-unguarded-driver-launch : NOT A DUPLICATE. It is the
        SAME handler/sink pair addressed by a DIFFERENT formulation. This query
        traverses CALL edges only and asserts nothing about data flow. The two
        are two formulations of one question, so their returns are reported side
        by side and NEVER SUMMED. This is a real question rather than a
        formality, precisely because the pair is shared: two queries over one
        pair are the case where a restatement would be easiest to mistake for a
        second, independent result.

      against 03-parameterized-handler-sink-pairs : NOT A DUPLICATE. A different
        target set and a different formulation - that query is parameterized over
        handler/sink pairs and covers a second pair this query does not address,
        the deploy/rest/StandaloneRestServer handler to the deploy/worker/
        DriverRunner sink.

    On what basis, stated plainly. The two other query sources are not present on
    this branch at the time this record is written, so the comparison rests on
    the formulation each query is defined to carry rather than on a diff of two
    files that exist. What IS measured here is this query's own formulation: CALL
    edges only, via NoResolve.getCalledMethodsAsTraversal at each call site, with
    no data-flow step anywhere and no parameterization over pairs.

  4.11 THE THREE EFFORT MEASURES, AS THIS QUERY ANSWERS THEM

